From 22c73c0a4a03c4790b46741afdce63b579fa2be4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fil Dunsky <filipp.dunsky@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 11:16:17 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 09/10] hw/arm/mt7981: MediaTek MT7986 (Filogic 830) machine,
 mt7986-router

The MT7986 shares most of its peripherals with the MT7981 (UART, IPM SPI,
SPI-NAND boot, TOPRGU, NETSYS v2 frame engine, SGMII, MT7531, xHCI,
WMAC), so the router machine is now parameterised by the SoC and
registered twice: mt7981-router and mt7986-router.

What differs on the MT7986:
- four Cortex-A53 cores (SPMC power-on for CPUs 2 and 3 too)
- HW_CODE 0x7986, pinctrl "gpio" block at 0x1001f000 with 101 pins,
  eFuse at 0x11d00000, two SPI controllers (a SPI-NOR boot flash is on
  SPI0), no built-in GbE PHY, MT7531 interrupt on EINT 66
- its BL2 (prebuilt DRAM library) detects the DRAM size with the DRAMC
  test agent: a pattern written at the size offset must wrap onto the
  base, so test runs beyond -m now compare wrong data
- its DQS gating sweep starts at another UI (12 for DDR4, 2 for DDR3):
  the modelled preamble is now placed relative to the start of the sweep
- frequency meter: the BL2 prints the CPU clock (2000 MHz)
- Wi-Fi: the mt7915 driver reads the a-die SKU back; one MT7976 (DBDC)
  is reported, so it loads the right EEPROM defaults and registers both
  bands (still radio silent)

Board hardware found on MT7986 routers:
- MaxLinear GPY211C 2.5G PHY (gmac0/gmac1/port5=gpy211): IDs, firmware
  version, MII status, mailbox and temperature sensor
- a 2.5G PHY on MT7531 port 5 (port5=, port5-phy-addr=, sixth entry of
  ports=), the switch then reports itself as MT7531AE (port 5 SGMII)

Tested with official OpenWrt 25.12.5 images (BL2, BL31, U-Boot, Linux,
LuCI): Xiaomi Redmi AX6000 (DDR4, WAN on the switch), Netcore N60 (DDR3,
2.5G WAN), Netcore N60 Pro (GPY211 on gmac1 and on switch port 5).
MT7981 boards boot as before.
